INT. THE BRANDT HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

The power cut hit forty minutes ago. A single candle gutters on the granite island. Rain runs down the dark windows. Somewhere in the neighbourhood, a generator coughs and gives up.

Colleen Brandt, 50s, still in her work lanyard, stands at the counter with a bottle of wine she can't open without light. Her husband's boss's wife has not yet arrived. Her son's Xbox sits uselessly on the table.

Walter Pryce, 40s, in a damp suit, comes in from the garage with a flashlight.

COLLEEN
You're late.

WALTER
There's a tree down on Maple. I walked.

COLLEEN
(a beat)
In those shoes.

WALTER
In these shoes.

He sets the flashlight on its end. It throws a pale circle at the ceiling. The refrigerator hums for a moment, then dies with a sigh.

Rain.

COLLEEN
I want to say something and I want you to let me finish.

WALTER
(beat)
Okay.

COLLEEN
Twenty-two years at Halvorsen. I trained every one of those people upstairs. I wrote the manual they pretend to follow. Last Thursday they put me in a room and a boy named Tyler, who is twenty-nine, told me my role was "being reimagined." He used the word "journey." He said I had been on a journey.

WALTER
Colleen...

COLLEEN
(beat)
I'm not finished. I smiled. I thanked him. I went to my desk and I alphabetised the supply cupboard because I did not know what else to do with my hands. And then I came home and made a lasagna that nobody ate, because the power went, and that's the whole of it.

The candle gutters. She finally puts the wine down.

This is the last evening either of them will pretend the house is fine.

WALTER
(quietly)
I got the same email.

Colleen turns. For the first time, she looks at him.
